r04: WebGL stability hardening (GLSL NaN audit, FBO lifecycle, context loss recovery)

Stability only. No visual restyling: colours, shapes and aesthetic parameter values are unchanged;
what changed is the maths form and the resource lifecycle.

A. GLSL static audit and repair

Built scripts/lint-glsl.mjs first, because "does it look right" cannot detect a NaN that only
appears on one pixel at one angle. Six rules: inverted smoothstep with literal bounds, integer pow,
unguarded pow, unguarded two-arg atan, and unclamped fragment output, plus an info-only rule for
smoothstep with runtime bounds. Exits 1 on any HIGH hit, so it can gate a commit.

Baseline before any fix: 33 HIGH across 8 shader files
  unclamped-fragcolor 13, unguarded-pow 9, integer-pow 5, inverted-smoothstep 4, unguarded-atan 2

Two of those were real NaN generators rather than theoretical:
  MasterNode3D L790  pow(centered.y, 2.0) - centered.y is negative on one side of the flare axis,
                     and pow(negative, 2.0) is undefined in GLSL. Rewritten as y*y, identical maths.
  WorkerRack3D L520  pow((r - 0.34) / 0.075, 2.0) - same negativeness on one side of the ring.
                     Split into a linear offset then squared.
  ClusterTopologyFloor3D L238/L923  atan(y, x) at the exact centre where both are 0 - undefined.
                     Now guarded by abs(x)+abs(y) > 1e-4.
  Also guarded uniform-valued divisors (uOuter-uInner, coreSigma) and the dynamic smoothstep
  bounds that could collapse to edge0 == edge1.

Also fixed the linter's own precision bug: it was flagging JS Math.pow inside GLSL-bearing files.
Math.pow accepts negative bases legitimately, so that rule must not apply there.

After: 0 HIGH, exit 0. Wired into phaseStatic so it runs in --quick too, and registered
pnpm lint:glsl.

B. Reflection and post-processing lifecycle (ClusterCanvas)

The actual instability was resolution={adaptiveDpr >= 1.5 ? 512 : 256}. MeshReflectorMaterial's
resolution sizes its RenderTarget; changing it at runtime tears down and rebuilds the FBO and
re-triggers post-processing size recalculation. That path leaks on some drivers and ends as
"gets slower and slower, then one black screen".

Now the tier is a module-level constant resolved once before first render (?quality= override, else
high), so the reflector RT is always the same number. Switching tiers remounts rather than mutating:
composer and reflector carry key={canvas-} and a 200 ms mask fades over the
rebuild.

PerformanceMonitor's response was inverted from what it should be. onDecline now only toggles
decorative layers (Sparkles, matrix rain) - never an FBO-affecting prop - because that is the
cheap, zero-rebuild lever. Any DPR change moved into onFallback behind three gates: frozen while
demoDirector status !== 'idle', max 2 adjustments per session, and >= 8 s between them;
monotonic down only, with no up-restore at all since bounce-back is itself the instability.

EffectComposer keeps multisampling={0} and now passes frameBufferType={HalfFloatType} explicitly.

For the demo freeze I used getDemoDirectorSnapshot() rather than the useDemoDirector hook on
purpose: subscribing would re-render the whole canvas on every 8 Hz demo progress tick.

C. Context loss recovery

onCreated binds webglcontextlost (with preventDefault, without which the browser never attempts
restore) and webglcontextrestored on gl.domElement. Lost shows a lightweight chip above the
viewport; restored bumps canvasRemountKey so the entire 3D tree is rebuilt, and logs
[GL] context restored. After 15 s without restore the chip switches to "please refresh" while the
2D HUD and drawer stay usable. Listeners and timers are removed on unmount - leaving them would
double-bind after remount and drive an infinite remount loop.

D. ?glDebug=1 NaN sentinel

Gated behind the URL flag, off by default and absent from production paths. Samples a 1x1 readPixels
at the canvas centre every 500 ms; three consecutive near-zero samples prints [GL-BLACKOUT] with the
store summary. Deliberately a setInterval, not a useFrame hook: readPixels inside useFrame forces a
pipeline sync and would destroy the frame rate it is trying to measure.

E. Regression

12 probe runs, 4 actions x 3 rounds, all clean:

  demo       0/3   chaos-oom  0/3   chaos-net  0/3   dpr-drop  0/3
  total 0/12 blackouts, all exit 0

tsc exit 0, next lint clean, lint-glsl exit 0 (0 HIGH), verify 33/33 (32 before, +1 for GLSL).

Honest limits recorded: the probe's effective sampling period is 2-3.5 s, so blackouts shorter than
about 3 s are still missed and 0/12 is a lower bound; and the ?glDebug 1x1 centre-pixel probe can
false-positive on a legitimately dark centre, which is why it is dev-only and tagged rather than
treated as a gate.

No dependencies added or upgraded.
